{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Michbad bad.</b> The vav is superfluous, like the vav of דוחיתו ארץ, בנו בעור בנו צפור. And do not wonder about the mem — if it is pointed with a hirik, since we find its parallel that it applies to the form מדותיו." "<b>Michbad bad.</b> They are the tunic, turban, and sash — all of them linen. Scripture expresses them in singular language, because all of them are one garment; he does not wear one without the other. And michnesav bad it writes separately, as we find in the section of תצוה. After it specified the garments of the high priest and the ordinary priest, it returns and explains the sash and the breeches for both of them. And in the Torat Kohanim it derives the four garments from the extra word ילבש — since it could have said ולבש מדו בד ומכנסי בד על בשרו, or if they were על בשרו, what does it say ילבש for? To include two other garments." "<b>Upon his flesh.</b> A euphemism for the private parts, like זב מבשרו, זובה בבשרה." "<b>And he shall lift up the ash.</b> The plain sense of the verse: when he wants to lift up the ash from upon the altar, he wears priestly garments — for one may not ascend the altar in lay garments." "<b>Et the ash.</b> From the ash, the full amount of a handful."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {1 "The derivation logic: since על בשרו already covers the breeches, the word ילבש is extra and serves to include the tunic and turban, yielding four garments total."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:10:13.413256114Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}